## Load Balancing WebSocket Connections

## The Problem

```
Naive round-robin:
  Client opens WS → Server A (OK)
  Client sends message → Load balancer → Server B (BROKEN — no connection)
```

WebSocket is stateful: the client's connection lives on ONE server.

## Solution 1: Sticky Sessions (IP Hash)

```nginx
upstream websocket {
  ip_hash;  # same client IP always routes to same server
  server ws1.example.com;
  server ws2.example.com;
  server ws3.example.com;
}

server {
  location /ws/ {
    proxy_pass http://websocket;
    proxy_http_version 1.1;
    proxy_set_header Upgrade $http_upgrade;
    proxy_set_header Connection "Upgrade";
  }
}
```

**Pros**: Simple  
**Cons**: Uneven load if some users are chatty; client loses connection if server dies

## Solution 2: Pub/Sub Message Bus (Scalable)

```javascript
// Server A handles Client 1
// Server B handles Client 2
// When Client 1 sends a message:

// Server A receives → publishes to Redis channel "room:general"
// Server B is subscribed → receives from Redis → pushes to Client 2

import { createClient } from 'redis';
const pub = createClient();
const sub = createClient();

// On message received from WebSocket client:
await pub.publish('room:general', JSON.stringify(message));

// All servers subscribe:
await sub.subscribe('room:general', (msg) => {
  // Push to all local WS clients in that room
  getLocalClients('general').forEach(ws => ws.send(msg));
});
```

**Pros**: True horizontal scaling, server failure is graceful  
**Cons**: Redis (or NATS) becomes a critical dependency

## Infrastructure Considerations

- Set long proxy timeouts (`proxy_read_timeout 3600s`)
- Configure heartbeat/ping-pong to detect dead connections
- Use WSS (WebSocket Secure) everywhere
- HAProxy, AWS ALB, and Nginx all support WebSocket proxying

